  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered thunderstorms expected again today.\n\nLow-level troughiness will remain in place through Tuesday and\nshould support development of widely scattered to scattered\nafternoon thunderstorms in an increasingly hot and humid air\nmass. The best chance for thunderstorms will be across the\nnorthern half of the fcst area where most recent NUCAPS data\nshowed the higher 850 mb dewpoint air and from potential \nthunderstorm clusters tracking southeastward in NW flow from \nPennsylvania into northern Maryland, but isolated thunderstorms\nare also possible over the higher terrain of the Blue Ridge \nMountains. Convective activity should wane quickly after sunset \nand dissipate by mid evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near-record early September heat expected.\n\nA strong mid-level ridge over the southern Plains will build toward \nthe Mid South during the early to middle part of the week. The \nlatest trend is for the ridge to build further east into the TN \nValley on Tuesday and Wednesday, then break down late in the week as \na strong trough digs down from southern Canada. Having the ridge \nbuild to the east is going to keep hot and mostly dry conditions \nover the region for longer.\n\nDespite the uncertainties, it remains likely that high temperatures \nwill reach the lower to middle 90s for most of the area this week. \nLatest guidance indicates potential for temperatures in the upper \n90s to near 100 by mid week, so long as it is not disrupted by cloud \ncover or convection. Depending on smaller scale details, dew points \ncould end up a little higher or lower than currently progged which \nwill ultimately affect how high heat index values get. Values \napproaching Heat Advisory criteria (100-104 west of the Blue Ridge, \n105-109 east of the Blue Ridge) are possible Tuesday through \nThursday. Similar heat and humidity could persist into Friday, but \nthere is a lot of uncertainty for the late week temperature forecast \ndue to possible convective systems moving through the area. \n\nTemperatures may begin to moderate by next weekend as a cold front \ncrosses the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Thunderstorm chances increase this week, but \ntiming remains highly uncertain.\n\nDisturbances rounding a ridge to the south and west in NW flow aloft \nwill introduce shower and thunderstorm chances at times this week. \nGiven the latest trend bringing the ridge further east during the \nmiddle of the week, that should keep most of the convective systems \nover NY/PA and the Northeast Tuesday and Wednesday. That does not \nmean we are completely in the clear those days; if anything, the \nhighest chance of seeing thunderstorms will be in northeast Maryland \nduring the late afternoon to evening as storms move south from PA. \nBy Thursday, a strong upper trough digs south from Canada which will \nrapidly break down the ridge overhead. As this occurs, our area is \ngoing to be in a much more favorable location for upstream \nshortwaves to pass overhead.\n\nThe enhanced lift and shear traversing the region besieged by \nseveral days of hot and humid conditions will introduce the \npotential for some significant severe weather. AI/ML guidance has \nbroad elevated probabilities of severe weather much of this week, \nparticularly Wednesday and Thursday. It should be noted that there \nis a conditional potential for one or more organized clusters of \nstrong to severe storms at some point this week. The most likely \ntime currently appears to be either Wednesday or Thursday, but \nchanges are likely given uncertainty at this range. It is quite \npossible given latest trends that the threat is highest Thursday and \nFriday as the upper ridge breaks down more readily.\n\nStorm chances may linger into next weekend as a cold front crosses \nthe region on Saturday.",
